@startuml D3_Flow4_Hooks
' ============================================================
' DOMAIN 3 — FLOW 4: Hooks
' Lifecycle events + cadence + blocking + exit codes +
' implementation + real-world applications (Topics 18, 21–23)
' CORE FRAME: group events by CADENCE (session/turn/tool);
'   ask "can it BLOCK or only OBSERVE?"
' EXAM #1 TRAP: exit 2 BLOCKS; exit 1 / any other = non-blocking
'   (the tool STILL RUNS). Hooks run at SHELL level, outside
'   Claude's context — cannot be prompted away.
' ============================================================
title Domain 3 · Flow 4 — Hooks (T18, 21–23)\n(Cadence: session/turn/tool · block vs observe · exit 2 blocks)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12
skinparam activityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF6CC

start
:A hook = a script YOU register to run at a named\nlifecycle event (register in settings.json, NOT CLAUDE.md);
#LightCyan:Runs at SHELL level, OUTSIDE Claude's context window;
note right
  == WHY HOOKS EXIST ==
  Claude can't see, override, or be prompted to skip a hook.
  Deterministic: fires EVERY time on every matching event.
  Prompt instructions can be rephrased-around or forgotten;
  a registered hook cannot. This is the hard security boundary.
end note

' ---------- CADENCE FRAME ----------
partition "T18 · Group events by CADENCE (outer->inner, slow->fast)" {
  #LightYellow:SESSION (once each): session start / session end;
  note right
    OBSERVE-ONLY (bookends). No single action to veto.
    session start = set up / load context on entry.
    session end   = tear down / clean up on exit.
  end note
  #LightYellow:TURN (once each): prompt submit / stop;
  note right
    BOTH can BLOCK.
    prompt submit (before Claude reads it) -> reject / reshape.
    stop = END-OF-TURN, NOT end-of-session; fires MANY times
    per session (once per agent turn). A blocking stop keeps
    the agent working. It sees the FULL result of the turn.
  end note
  #LightYellow:TOOL (every call): pre-tool-use / post-tool-use;
  note right
    Fire MANY times per turn (wrap each tool the agent runs).
    pre-tool-use  = fires BEFORE the tool -> CAN BLOCK (the gate)
    post-tool-use = fires AFTER  the tool -> CANNOT block
                    (already ran) -> react / log / format / test
    "Pre guards. Post reacts." Prevention ALWAYS needs pre-tool-use.
    Special: pre-compact (can block, protect from summarising);
    notification (observe-only, ROUTES alerts out).
    ~30 events exist; you need the 4 common + the cadence frame.
  end note
}

' ---------- BLOCK vs OBSERVE decision ----------
partition "The One Question: BLOCK or OBSERVE?" {
  if (Do you need to STOP an action?) then (YES = gate)
    #LightGreen:Pick a BLOCKING event (pre-tool-use / stop / pre-compact / prompt-submit);
  else (NO = record/alert)
    #LightBlue:Pick an OBSERVE-ONLY event (post-tool-use / session / notification);
    note right
      Keeps the hook out of the critical path.
      notification's ONLY job = route alerts to Slack/PagerDuty/
      webhook. It CANNOT block. Exam pairing:
      "block an action" -> ALWAYS pre-tool-use;
      "surface an alert externally" -> notification. Never mix.
    end note
  endif
}

' ---------- EXIT CODES (the #1 trap) ----------
partition "T21–22 · Exit Codes (highest-frequency trap)" {
  if (Hook exit code?) then (0)
    #LightGreen:CONTINUE — action proceeds, structured output read;
  elseif (2) then
    #Pink:BLOCK — action stopped, stderr fed back to Claude as the reason;
    note right
      ONLY exit 2 blocks (for events allowed to block).
    end note
  else (1 or any other)
    #Orange:NON-BLOCKING error — logged, but the tool STILL RUNS;
    note right
      == THE #1 TRAP (both directions) ==
      * Want to enforce a block but return 1 -> it does NOT block.
      * Accidental exit 2 in a LOGGING hook -> blocks EVERY call;
        looks exactly like "Claude refusing to act" — but the
        real cause is a misconfigured exit code.
      Fails SILENTLY -> always test every exit path first.
    end note
  endif
}

' ---------- IMPLEMENTATION ----------
partition "T22 · Implementing Hooks (settings, scripts, gotchas)" {
  :Register in settings.json: event + command/script path;
  note right
    Multiple hooks on one event run IN PARALLEL (don't assume
    ordering); identical handlers deduped.
    Pre-tool-use gets call context as JSON on stdin
    (session id, cwd, event, tool name, tool input).
    Inline one-liners for trivial logging; EXTERNAL SCRIPT for
    anything with logic (testable, version-controlled, reviewable).
    ----
    3 GOTCHAS (all fail silently):
    1. accidental exit 2 -> blocks everything
    2. slow hook -> every tool call waits on it (HOOK TIMEOUT);
       slow/unreachable service stalls the session
    3. wrong event type (post-tool-use when you needed pre)
    3 SAFETY PATTERNS:
    * allow-list (unknown = blocked; conservative default)
    * block-list (name the dangerous; unknown implicitly allowed)
    * dry-run (log what it WOULD block; enforce once confident)
  end note
}

' ---------- APPLICATIONS ----------
partition "T23 · Real-World Hook Applications (pattern -> hook type)" {
  #LightGreen:Match each pattern to its lifecycle event;
  note right
    POST-tool-use : auto-format (Black/Prettier after write),
                    run tests after edit (scope to src/ paths),
                    audit log, cost/token tracking
    PRE-tool-use  : safety net — block rm -rf / DROP TABLE (exit 2)
    STOP          : notify Slack when a long batch job finishes
    ----
    LOGGING NUANCE (subtle, tested):
    pre-tool-use log = INTENT (captures BLOCKED attempts too)
      -> good for catching policy violations.
    post-tool-use log = RESULTS (what actually succeeded).
    Many teams run BOTH for a complete intent->execution picture.
  end note
}

stop

floating note left
  == FLOW 4 SPINE ==
  Hook = shell-level script at a named event (outside context) ->
  place it by CADENCE (session/turn/tool) -> ask BLOCK or OBSERVE ->
  enforce with EXIT 2 -> register in settings.json -> match the
  application pattern to pre/post/stop/notification.
  ----
  == HIGH-YIELD FACTS ==
  * Runs at SHELL level; can't be prompted away; fires every time
  * Cadence nesting: session (observe) / turn (block) / tool (pre=block, post=observe)
  * "stop" = end-of-TURN, fires many times per session
  * notification = route only, never blocks
  * exit 0 = go · exit 2 = BLOCK · exit 1/other = non-blocking (runs anyway)
  * register in settings.json; multiple hooks run in parallel
  * pre-log = intent (incl. blocked) · post-log = results
end note

@enduml
